d him  including himself now on the topic of  charity and dwindling wealth the themes  of money greed poverty and revolution  are very much a driving force  particularly with the film's secondary  characters and main villain the  wonderful selena kyle makes her nolan  debut here in this picture and i love  anne hathaway's portrayal of the  character the focus isn't on her sexual  awakening like catwoman 2004 but it's  instead concentrating on her position in  the world and the moral code she has the  perspective she possesses speak a lot to  the themes of inequality in the city of  gotham and the world at large her seeing  bruce wayne is fair game to steal from  as he has the means to support himself  regardless she's a criminal yes but she  is principled the same can be said for  vayne now we don't get to see much of  him outside of the first half of the  film but we do feel his presence while  not being the hulking mindless gross  mess we saw in the late 90s  interpretation of the character if you  could even call it that in this he  proves to us why he's one of batman's  most terrifying villains following films  of the dark knight which had heath  ledger's career defining performance in  the joker a lesser director might have  looked to recapture that same lightning  in a battle by trying to recreate the  magic with a different character or  actor no such choice happens here  thankfully the dark knight rises feels  very much like a labor of love with a  particular fondness for the various  idiosyncrasies that make bane such an  interesting character there is a batman  comic series called nightfall produced  during the early 90s and for the most  part this is the de facto bane story  that put him on the map as one of the  most intimidating and terrifying enemies  of the dark knight all the while  managing to tell a grounded story about  batman particularly his psychology in  relation to banes and is not only widely  praised and acclaimed but for the  purposes of t