ade by the audio  mixing team but bane's voice always  sounded like it was eerily close to me  like it cut through every scene's  atmosphere the action in this film on  the other hand isn't good at all and  i'll say that here to avoid having to  address it later the way it's shot feels  almost amateur compared to the rest of  the film not helped at all by the  choreography which feels more like a bad  power rangers episode rather than a  summer blockbuster of this caliber  however and this is a big however the  first fight against bane is helped a lot  i think by this very weird action and  i'll explain why batman appears weak and  pathetic against bane who barely tries  there's no pageantry there's no illusion  of intimidation the camera work is  intentionally flaccid with bane's men  watching this unravel before their rise  catwoman included his suit clearly  visible cape soaked weighing him down  this all serves one purpose to make  batman appear pathetic totally  outmatched and unprepared and it works  alfred was right  now it's time to rebuild and once again  we get another great sequence of scenes  as bane brings the city of gotham to its  knees without anyone to stand in his way  and we get bruce wayne a beat man  clinging to life  learning how to walk again forgotten at  the bottom of a dungeon in a well unable  to escape  well that is until he does  rise of the dark knight  there's an important change that happens  with bruce wayne in this sequence batman  now is fighting to live instead of  waiting to die what i love about this  story is that it truly feels like one  long continuous narrative across all  three films the reincorporation of  characters like razal ghoul coupled with  the training he needs to undertake in  order to lift himself from this literal  and metaphorical pit of despair harkens  back to the days of batman begins in  other words this truly feels like a  complete series with a singular  uncompromising vision it's spectacular  however i can't help but feel a powerful  sense of what could have been this film  is a powerful reflection on the past  characters like scarecrow reclaim roles  with new positions of power and they are  massively entertaining having discovered  through my research for this video that  he had planned on performing in this  film also makes me wonder in what  capacity would the joker have been  involved in the final product a  character the likes of which is obsessed  with anarchy and chaos would have been  an ideal candidate for the world like  this and so nostalgically i do look back  with sadness but as soon as i do this  film utilizes nostalgia in a positive  way that too serves the story the  resurgence of quotes like bruce why do  we fall haunting his dreams as he fails  to scale out of this pit to rescue the  people he swore to protect serves to  create a sense of occasion and purpose  the likes of which a single movie cannot  on its own accomplish with the final  climbing attempt out of this pit  possessing a powerful philosophy the  likes of which i wasn't expecting though  it is perfect for this occasion for  months as bruce has agonized in this  dungeon he's heard the tale of a child  that has managed to drag himself out of  the darkness a feat no ordinary child or  adult for that matter could perform a  child forged from suffering and pain who  we learned climbed that wall without the  use of a rope the interesting part of  this film isn't necessarily how weak  bruce appeared or how he ultimately  bails himself back up from that but how  bane thoroughly broke his spirit as well  as his back this film explores what it  means to fight fully with mind body and  spirit and questions what spirit in  combat means and how one could expect to  fight with full spirit if they do not  fear death how can they run faster fight  longer than possible how can they expect  to try harder than someone that is  scared to die which honestly is an  interesting concept but when the  solution to this problem was proposed i  myself got scared you see over christmas  i watched a film called free solo and as  the name suggests it follows one man by  the name of alex honold climbing a cliff  face called el capitan in the yosemite  national park in california without a  rope harness or anything and one thing  this film does a great job of is  granting you a perspective on how one  might feel while undertaking such a  challenge which honestly helped me  immensely in understanding exactly the  impulse and mindset bruce was trying to  achieve here i really like also that  upon his return he announces his  presence to the city not with the bat  signal but with a bright fiery symbol  scorched onto the bridge piercing  through the darkness of the night this  idea of batman as a symbol for the city  of gotham is something i truly  appreciated while watching these films  back to back this symbol plays a role in  every film whether it be him  establishing it in the first one he's  reinforcing that ideal in the dark  knight or right now with him standing by  those same virtues to the bitter end in  the dark knight rises it acts as a  powerful through line with all of these  films that in the moment brought a tear  to my eye the other desperation these  cops must have been feeding their  friends and families terrorized or worse  and now with only hours standing between  them and certain death they stand before  bane's army shaking with fear in their  eyes  seeing batman revitalize their morale  with his presence is exactly what i came  to this movie for and boy does it  deliver  the ending i'm not gonna lie having been  so caught up in the moment of bruce  fighting one-on-one against bane in the  rematch to end all rematches when  miranda stabbed him in the back and  announced that she was the one who  escaped the daughter of razakul i lost  my collective [ __ ] a planned three  movies in the making a plan to destroy  gotham completely finally realizing her  father's mission and i love that joker  or no joker this is a top class film  that is so damn entertaining from start  to finish the switcheroo with the main  villain and her backstory the reveal of  robin who is brilliant in the movie by  the way gordon and batman sharing a  super personal moment together and the  final carrying away of the bomb to a  safe distance there's a wonderful yet  haunting sense of foreboding finality to  this ending and furthermore i don't  think i felt a more sorrowful scene  consisting of just one man's reaction  than i did with alfred's breakdown in  front of the three headstones of the  wayne family apologizing profusely to  tom and martha for not being enough to  save their little boy and i'm not afraid  to admit it this really got it got me  good  i'm not sure what the feelings  surrounding this film's ending are but  for me this was as close to a perfect  ending for this trilogy that i could  imagine i have quite literally no issues  narratively and what's more is that the  surprises it's filled with were not only  previously established but they feed  into the motivations and themes of these  films perfectly i cannot describe to you  the whirlwind of emotions i felt as it  was explained that an autopilot system  had been installed into the back craft  or geez when alfred sat down in that  restaurant a scene established at the  beginning of this film perfectly in line  with the mechanics of what was  established with the clean slate tool  provided to selena the motivation of  batman to finally move on to bigger and  better things in life and of course  alfred finally getting closure for his  perceived failings in raising his dear  friend's only child bruce wayne it's a  powerful ending full of catharsis and i  for one am over the moon that this is  where christian bale's batman was  finally laid to rest at no point did  this film trilogy ease up or rest on its  laurels at no point did it coast or rely  on the brand of batman to carry it forth  onto the global zeitgeist this trilogy  is a work of art produced by a team of  visionaries that understood the  characters on an intimate level a level  i felt that only now having delved into  the comics as deep as i have could fully  appreciate this will not be the last  time i see these films and to anyone out  there who hasn't revisited these films  or has seen them at all please go fix  that as soon as you can this is my  favorite story of the trilogy and of any  batman film i've seen over this long  journey and while the action in this  movie leaves a lot to be desired and its  antagonists weren't as iconic as heath  ledger's joker i am still willing to  give this film a 9.5 out of 10. it's  just that damn good next week i'll be  tackling my final batman video where i  review the dc universe films with batman  and i will finally watch the new batman  movie starring robert pattinson but  until then i've been totally not mark  i'll see you all next weekend  thank you all so much for watching  no that was a big video for you  there it is  [Music]  you
 

